window.CS_NARR=window.CS_NARR||{};window.CS_NARR["ovsm"]={"ovsmatlas": [[null, ["Hi, Buddy here. This sheet covers the basics of the Indian stock market, condensed from OpenAlgo's free Open Varsity course. It goes from why you should invest at all, through how the market is wired, how companies list, how to read a price, what moves prices, and finally how to start safely. One honest note before we begin: this is education, not investment advice, and the figures on the sheet are approximate and change over time. Let's start with the map."]], ["stock-market-basics-topic-atlas", ["Here's why the sheet runs in this order. First, why invest at all and what you're buying, then the market ecosystem that keeps your money safe. Going public explains where shares come from and why prices jump on corporate actions. Reading the market decodes quotes and indices, and what moves prices explains the news. Becoming an investor comes last: your first steps, the biases to watch, and how to stay safe."]], ["how-to-use-this-sheet", ["How to use this sheet depends on where you are. If you're brand new, read money and markets and then the ecosystem first; they explain what you're actually buying and who keeps it safe. If you're about to open an account, jump to becoming an investor for KYC, the beginner roadmap and the index SIP versus direct stock decision. If a price on your screen confuses you, the reading the market section decodes it, including why prices jump after corporate actions. And before you act on any tip, run the scam checklist near the end."]], ["the-investor-s-journey", ["This flow is the investor's journey, and each step is a gate. First understand why you invest: to beat inflation. Then build an emergency fund and clear costly debt. Then open a demat and trading account and finish KYC. Then start an index fund SIP. Then learn about the businesses you own, add direct stocks slowly, and review quarterly rather than daily. The important point is the order. Most beginners who get hurt skipped the early, boring steps: the cash cushion, paying off debt, and building a habit.", "The takeaway: Each step is a gate: beat inflation, build a cushion, open accounts, start an index SIP, learn, then add stocks slowly and review quarterly.", "Pause and answer: What comes before opening a demat account?", "⏸", "Answer: An emergency fund and no costly debt."]], ["four-questions-before-any-investment", ["Four questions to ask before any investment, and each one maps to a part of this sheet. One: does my money beat inflation after tax? Idle cash loses buying power every year. Two: who actually holds my shares and guarantees the trade? The answer is the depositories and the clearing corporation, not your broker. Three: is a price move real, or just an adjustment? Bonus, split and dividend drops are arithmetic, not losses. And four: is the person advising me registered with SEBI? Unregistered paid tips are illegal and usually a trap.", "The takeaway: Does it beat inflation after tax, who holds the shares, is the move real or an adjustment, and could you hold through a big fall?", "Pause and answer: Who actually holds your shares and guarantees the trade?", "⏸", "Answer: The depository and the clearing corporation, not your broker."]]], "money": [[null, ["Section one: money and markets. Why invest at all, where money can go, and what a share actually is."]], ["inflation-and-real-returns", ["Inflation quietly shrinks what each rupee buys. At about 6% a year, ₹1 lakh kept idle from the year 2000 buys only what about ₹23,000 bought then, roughly 25 years later. Saving protects the rupee amount; investing aims to protect and grow your buying power. You need both: a cash cushion and growing assets. A savings account paying about 3% against 5 to 6% inflation is a slow leak. The formula on the card is the real return: roughly the nominal return minus inflation. A fixed deposit at 7% with 6% inflation earns about 1% real. Equity at around 12% earns about 6% real. So always judge returns after inflation, and after tax, never by the headline number.", "The takeaway: Inflation quietly erodes buying power; real return ≈ nominal return − inflation.", "Pause and answer: Savings at 3% with 5–6% inflation: what's the real return?", "⏸", "Answer: Negative, about −2 to −3% a year: a slow leak."]], ["compounding-and-starting-early", ["Compounding rewards time more than anything else. Take a ₹5,000 a month SIP growing at about 12% a year. After 10 years you've put in ₹6 lakh and it's worth about ₹11.6 lakh. After 20 years, ₹12 lakh in, about ₹50 lakh out. After 30 years, ₹18 lakh in, about ₹1.76 crore. Tripling the time multiplied the result about 15 times, because the last years do the most work. Here's the striking example: someone who invests ₹5,000 a month from age 25 to 35 and then stops completely ends up at 60 with more than someone who invests the same amount from 35 all the way to 60, despite putting in less than half the money. The lesson: time in the market beats timing the market.", "The takeaway: Time does most of the work: tripling the years multiplied the result about 15 times, and the last years matter most.", "Pause and answer: ₹5,000 a month at about 12% for 30 years grows to roughly what?", "⏸", "Answer: About ₹1.76 crore, on ₹18 lakh invested."]], ["the-six-asset-classes-compared", ["The six asset classes. Cash and savings are instantly spendable, very low risk, and earn about 3 to 4%, with interest taxed at your slab. Debt, meaning FDs and bonds, is lending for fixed interest: low risk, about 6 to 7%, also taxed at slab. Gold is a scarce metal and crisis hedge: medium risk, about 8 to 10% over the long run, and 12.5% long term tax after 24 months for ETFs and funds. Real estate offers 7 to 9% plus rent, but very low liquidity. Equity is part ownership of businesses: high short term risk, around 12% long run, with 12.5% tax on long term gains above ₹1.25 lakh a year and 20% on short term gains. And crypto is digital tokens with no cash flows: very high risk, extreme swings, and a flat 30% tax with 1% TDS per sale and no loss set off. Mutual funds and ETFs are just baskets of these building blocks. Remember: higher potential return always comes with higher risk, and risk means a wider range of outcomes, not certain loss. Tax rates change with each Budget, so check the current ones.", "The takeaway: Cash, debt, gold, real estate, equity and crypto differ in risk, return, liquidity and tax; equity has the highest long-run return.", "Pause and answer: Which asset class has historically given the highest long-run return?", "⏸", "Answer: Equity."]], ["asset-allocation-basics", ["Asset allocation means spreading money across classes so one can offset another. A rough starting rule: your equity share is about 100 minus your age, so a 25 year old might hold about 75% equity and the rest in debt and a little gold. It's a starting point, not a law. Keep the emergency fund in cash or liquid funds, never in property. Keep money you'll need within 3 to 5 years out of equities. Why does equity get the biggest slice for long horizons? Because it owns growing businesses that reinvest their profits. The Sensex went from a base of 100 in 1979 to roughly 76,000 as the course gives it, so check today's level, about 770 times in around 45 years.", "The takeaway: Spread across classes; a rough starting rule is equity ≈ 100 − age, and money needed within 3–5 years stays out of equity.", "Pause and answer: Rough equity share for a 25-year-old by the rule of thumb?", "⏸", "Answer: About 75%."]], ["recent-asset-class-notes", ["A few recent notes. Gold rose about 27% in 2024 and surged further in 2025, with international gold above 4,000 dollars an ounce; but a strong run doesn't guarantee a repeat. Sovereign Gold Bonds had no new tranches after the 2025 Budget; existing bonds keep paying 2.5% a year until maturity, and new buyers use gold ETFs or gold funds instead. And crypto can swing 10% in a day and has fallen more than 70% from its peaks in past cycles. It pays no profits or dividends, so it should never be the foundation of your wealth.", "The takeaway: Gold ran hard in 2024–25, SGB tranches stopped, and crypto can swing wildly and pays no profits; a strong run guarantees nothing.", "Pause and answer: Can you still buy new Sovereign Gold Bonds from the government?", "⏸", "Answer: No new tranches after the 2025 Budget; new buyers use gold ETFs or funds."]], ["what-a-share-actually-is", ["A share is legal part ownership of a company. Your stake is your shares divided by the total shares outstanding. It gives you rights: a claim on profits through dividends, a claim on what's left after debts if the company winds up, and usually one vote per share at the annual general meeting. You earn in two ways: price appreciation, called capital gains, and dividends while you hold. Prices come from a continuous auction. When there are more eager buyers than sellers, the price rises, and the displayed price is simply the latest agreed trade. And notice that the price per share says nothing about value. MRF trades above ₹1.5 lakh a share simply because it has never split its shares, not because it's expensive.", "The takeaway: A share is part-ownership: a claim on profits and residual assets, usually with a vote; you earn from price gains and dividends.", "Pause and answer: How do you calculate your ownership stake?", "⏸", "Answer: Your shares ÷ shares outstanding."]], ["debt-vs-equity-primary-vs-secondary", ["Two distinctions. Debt versus equity: debt is an obligation to pay interest and repay, so the company can default, but it doesn't dilute the owners. Equity has no obligation and no default risk for the company, but issuing it dilutes existing owners. Then primary versus secondary market. In the primary market, the company itself sells new shares and the cash goes to the company, as in an IPO. In the secondary market, you buy from another investor, and the cash goes to that seller, as when you buy TCS on NSE or BSE. The formulas at the bottom: dividend income equals dividend per share times shares held, and capital gain equals sell price minus buy price.", "The takeaway: Debt is a fixed obligation, equity is ownership; IPO money goes to the company, secondary-market money to another investor.", "Pause and answer: Buying TCS on NSE: where does your money go?", "⏸", "Answer: To the selling investor (secondary market), not to TCS."]]], "eco": [[null, ["Section two: the market ecosystem. The regulators, the plumbing, and how your trade actually settles."]], ["sebi-and-rbi-who-guards-what", ["Two regulators matter most. SEBI watches shares, bonds, mutual funds and derivatives. Its jobs are to protect investors, to register and regulate exchanges, brokers, funds and advisers, and to develop the market, for example with faster settlement, demat holding and better disclosure. The RBI looks after the rupee and the banks: it issues currency, fights inflation through the repo rate, regulates banks and manages foreign exchange. SEBI's investor protection toolkit includes bans on insider trading, manipulation and misleading ads; mandatory disclosure; shares held in your own demat account; fines and bans; and the SCORES complaint portal. Keep in mind that SEBI registration signals accountability, not guaranteed profits.", "The takeaway: SEBI regulates securities markets and protects investors; RBI manages the rupee, banks and inflation through the repo rate.", "Pause and answer: Which regulator sets the repo rate?", "⏸", "Answer: The RBI."]], ["reforms-usually-follow-scandals", ["In India, reforms usually follow scandals. SEBI was formed in 1988 as an advisory body with few powers. The 1992 Harshad Mehta scam led to the SEBI Act giving it statutory powers. The 2001 Ketan Parekh scam brought tighter settlement and the ban on badla. The 2009 Satyam fraud triggered a corporate governance overhaul. In 2024 and 2025, new finfluencer rules stopped registered entities from tying up with unregistered finfluencers. And SEBI's study for FY25 found about 91% of individual F and O traders lost money, which led to fewer weekly expiries and bigger lot sizes.", "The takeaway: Indian market reforms usually followed scandals, from SEBI's statutory powers in 1992 to curbs on unregistered finfluencers.", "Pause and answer: What gave SEBI statutory powers?", "⏸", "Answer: The SEBI Act of 1992, after the Harshad Mehta scam."]], ["exchanges-brokers-depositories-clearing", ["Four players make the market work. The exchange matches orders and publishes trades, but doesn't set prices; India has BSE, founded in 1875 and Asia's oldest, and NSE, founded in 1994 and the largest by volume. The broker is a licensed member that routes your orders, either discount or full service. The depository holds your shares electronically: NSDL, started in 1996, and CDSL. Your broker acts as your depository participant, the link to the depository, and charges about ₹13 to ₹20 per sell that leaves your demat. And the clearing corporation guarantees both sides of every trade. In one line: exchanges match, brokers route, depositories hold, clearing corporations guarantee. Your shares sit at NSDL or CDSL, not with your broker, which is why a broker failing doesn't take your shares with it.", "The takeaway: Exchanges match orders, brokers route them, depositories hold shares, and the clearing corporation guarantees settlement.", "Pause and answer: Does the exchange set prices?", "⏸", "Answer: No. It matches orders and publishes trades."]], ["trading-hours-and-the-closing-auction", ["Trading hours, Monday to Friday, Indian time. The pre open session runs from 9 to 9:15, where a call auction sets the opening price. Normal trading runs from 9:15 to 3:30 in the afternoon. A post close session from 3:40 to 4 lets you trade at the closing price. Orders placed while the market is shut wait as after market orders and go in at the next open, and at Diwali there's a one hour Muhurat session. A big change: from the 3rd of August 2026, stocks that have derivatives close through a closing auction. Continuous trading stops at 3:15. The reference price is the volume weighted average price from 3:00 to 3:15. Order entry closes at a random moment between 3:28 and 3:30, and matching ends by 3:35, within plus or minus 3% of the reference. Other stocks keep the old close, the volume weighted price of the last 30 minutes. And equity derivatives aren't in the auction: they trade until 3:40 and close on the volume weighted price from 3:10 to 3:40.", "The takeaway: Pre-open 9:00–9:15, normal market 9:15 to 3:30 pm, post-close 3:40–4:00 pm; a closing auction applies to F&O stocks from August 2026.", "Pause and answer: What sets the opening price?", "⏸", "Answer: The pre-open call auction from 9:00 to 9:15."]], ["brokers-and-the-true-cost-of-a-trade", ["Discount versus full service brokers. A discount broker offers a platform only, charges a flat fee of about ₹20 per order, often zero on delivery trades, and suits self directed investors. A full service broker adds research and advice, usually charges a percentage of the trade value, and suits investors who want guidance. But every trade also pays statutory charges: securities transaction tax, exchange charges, 18% GST on brokerage and charges, stamp duty on buys, a SEBI fee, and DP charges on sells. So \"zero brokerage\" isn't zero cost. On tiny trades, the statutory charges can be more than the brokerage itself. One update: from the 7th of December 2026, after a SEBI review, the schedule changes again. Continuous trading runs to 3:30, the closing auction runs from 3:30 to 3:40, and stock and index derivatives trade until 3:45, except index contracts expiring that day, which stop at 3:30.", "The takeaway: Brokerage is only part of the cost; STT, exchange charges, GST, stamp duty, SEBI fees and DP charges add up.", "Pause and answer: Discount versus full-service broker: what's the core difference?", "⏸", "Answer: Discount brokers offer a platform at a flat fee; full-service add research and advice, usually at a percentage."]], ["demat-account-types", ["Demat account types. Regular accounts for residents, plus joint, corporate, custodial for minors through a guardian, and premium accounts for frequent traders. NRIs have two kinds: repatriable, linked to an NRE account, and non repatriable, linked to an NRO account. There's also BSDA, the basic services demat account: no annual charge up to ₹4 lakh of holdings and a small fee up to ₹10 lakh, but it's only allowed if it's your single demat account. For scale, India passed about 21.6 crore demat accounts by December 2025, about 17 crore at CDSL and 4 crore at NSDL, though NSDL holds more by value, and far fewer accounts are active in any year.", "The takeaway: Regular, joint, corporate, custodial, NRI repatriable and non-repatriable, and BSDA for small holdings.", "Pause and answer: What's a BSDA?", "⏸", "Answer: A basic services demat account: no annual charge up to ₹4 lakh of holdings."]], ["from-buy-click-to-shares-in-demat", ["This flow shows what happens after you click buy. You place an order. Your broker routes it. The exchange matches you with a seller. The clearing corporation steps in as the counterparty to both sides. Netting works out one net obligation per security. Settlement happens on T plus 1, with the pay in and pay out. And the shares land in your demat. Remember the three words: a trade is the matched promise; clearing works out who owes what; and settlement actually moves money and shares, both legs at once, so neither side has to trust the other.", "The takeaway: Order, broker, exchange match, clearing as counterparty, netting, T+1 settlement, shares in demat.", "Pause and answer: Who becomes the counterparty after the exchange matches your trade?", "⏸", "Answer: The clearing corporation."]], ["settlement-cycles-and-t-0", ["How settlement got faster. Until 2001 India used weekly badla settlement. It moved to T plus 3 in 2002, then T plus 2 in 2003, which stayed the global norm for two decades. In January 2023 India fully adopted T plus 1, ahead of the US, which followed in May 2024. In 2024 and 2025, an optional T plus 0 beta was phased in across the top 500 stocks, about 100 at a time. Under T plus 1, sale proceeds are usable and purchases are delivered the next working day. T plus 0 runs alongside and is optional, and note that it's about when ownership is final, not about intraday trading.", "The takeaway: India moved from weekly badla to T+3, T+2 and full T+1 by January 2023; optional T+0 runs alongside for top stocks.", "Pause and answer: When did India fully adopt T+1?", "⏸", "Answer: January 2023, before the US (May 2024)."]], ["why-trades-almost-never-fail", ["Why do trades almost never fail in India? Four safeguards. Novation: the clearing corporation becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer, which removes counterparty risk. Margins: traders deposit money upfront as a cushion. The Settlement Guarantee Fund: a pool that completes settlement if a member defaults. And auctions and penalties: if a seller fails to deliver, shares are bought in the market for the buyer and the defaulter pays. Netting also reduces what has to move: buy 10 and sell 5 of the same stock in a day, and only the net 5 shares actually move.", "The takeaway: Novation, margins, a settlement guarantee fund and auctions for failed deliveries make settlement nearly certain.", "Pause and answer: What is novation?", "⏸", "Answer: The clearing corporation becoming buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer."]]], "ipo": [[null, ["Section three: going public. How companies raise money, how IPOs work, and what corporate actions do to your holdings."]], ["the-funding-ladder", ["A company climbs a funding ladder. It starts as an idea, gets bootstrapped by the founders, then raises from friends, family and angels, then venture capital in seed, Series A, B and C rounds, then private equity, and finally an IPO and listing. Each rung usually brings bigger cheques at higher valuations. Angels write about ₹25 lakh to a few crore, VC rounds run from crores to hundreds of crores, and private equity from hundreds to thousands of crores. A unicorn is a startup valued at a billion dollars or more, and India has well over 100 of them.", "The takeaway: Bootstrapping, angels, venture capital, private equity, then the IPO: each rung brings bigger cheques at higher valuations.", "Pause and answer: Which round comes before private equity on the ladder?", "⏸", "Answer: Venture capital (seed, Series A, B, C)."]], ["valuation-and-dilution", ["Valuation and dilution. The implied valuation is the investment divided by the stake acquired: ₹10 crore for 20% means ₹10 crore divided by 0.2, a ₹50 crore valuation. The founder's stake value is the valuation times their percentage. Follow the table. Bootstrapped at ₹2 crore with 100%, the founder's stake is worth ₹2 crore. At seed, ₹20 crore valuation and 75%: ₹15 crore. Series A, ₹100 crore and 55%: ₹55 crore. Series B, ₹500 crore and 40%: ₹200 crore. And at IPO, ₹4,000 crore and 30%: ₹1,200 crore. The lesson is that a smaller slice of a much bigger company can be worth far more. But private valuations are negotiated opinions, not guarantees of IPO returns.", "The takeaway: Implied valuation = investment ÷ stake; a founder's shrinking slice of a bigger company can be worth far more.", "Pause and answer: ₹10 crore buys 20%. Implied valuation?", "⏸", "Answer: ₹50 crore."]], ["why-companies-go-public", ["Why do companies go public? Capital: they can raise hundreds or thousands of crores for growth or to repay debt. Liquidity: early investors and employees can finally sell. Visibility: listing builds trust with customers, lenders and potential hires. The cost is quarterly disclosure, public scrutiny, and a share price that the market judges every single day.", "The takeaway: Capital, liquidity and visibility, paid for with quarterly disclosure and daily public judgement.", "Pause and answer: Name two benefits of going public.", "⏸", "Answer: Any two of: capital, liquidity for early holders, visibility and trust."]], ["how-an-ipo-works", ["Here's the IPO process. The company files a draft red herring prospectus, the DRHP, with SEBI. Then comes the red herring prospectus with the price band and dates. Book building runs for about three days of bids. The cut off price is fixed. Allotment follows, by lottery if the issue is oversubscribed; unallotted money is unblocked; and then listing day, when trading starts. A few points to know. A fresh issue means new shares and money goes to the company; an offer for sale means existing holders are selling, and the money goes to them. Check the mix in the offer document. You apply in lots through ASBA or UPI, and the money is blocked, not debited, until allotment. Retail allotment is a random draw of one lot per applicant, so applying for more lots doesn't raise your chance of getting the first lot. And the grey market premium, GMP, is unofficial, unregulated rumour, not a reason to apply.", "The takeaway: DRHP, RHP with price band, book-building bids, cut-off price, allotment by lottery if oversubscribed, then listing.", "Pause and answer: Fresh issue versus OFS: where does the money go?", "⏸", "Answer: Fresh issue money goes to the company; OFS money goes to selling shareholders."]], ["ipo-categories-and-what-to-read", ["IPO categories. Qualified institutional buyers, meaning mutual funds, banks, insurers and foreign portfolio investors, get up to 50% of the issue. Non institutional investors, individuals applying above ₹2 lakh, get at least 15%. Retail, up to ₹2 lakh, gets at least 35%. Anchor investors can take up to 60% of the institutional part a day early; half their shares are locked in for 30 days and half for 90. When you read the red herring prospectus, read the risk factors first, then the objects of the issue, the financials, promoter holding, and the basis for the price compared with listed peers. And SME IPOs on NSE Emerge and BSE SME have lighter rules, higher minimum investments and much higher volatility; they're not for first timers.", "The takeaway: QIBs up to 50%, NIIs at least 15%, retail at least 35%; read risk factors first in the RHP.", "Pause and answer: What is the retail investor limit per application?", "⏸", "Answer: Up to ₹2 lakh."]], ["listing-day-is-not-a-promise", ["Listing day is not a promise. The listing gain percentage is the listing price minus the issue price, divided by the issue price, times 100. An issue at ₹70 that lists at ₹150 gains about 114%. But big brands can list flat or lower: India's largest IPO to date listed about 1% below its issue price. And big listing pops can fade: one 2024 issue more than doubled on day one, then fell about a quarter over the following months. For context, 2024 saw about 91 mainboard IPOs raising roughly ₹1.6 lakh crore.", "The takeaway: Listing gains are not guaranteed: big brands can list flat or lower, and big pops can fade.", "Pause and answer: Issue ₹70, lists at ₹150. Listing gain?", "⏸", "Answer: About +114%."]], ["corporate-actions-at-a-glance", ["Corporate actions at a glance. A rights issue creates new shares if you subscribe; money goes to the company, the price adjusts down slightly, and you're neutral if you subscribe or sell your entitlement. An FPO, a follow on offer, creates new shares and can make the price dip on dilution. An OFS, offer for sale, creates no new shares; money goes to the selling holders, and there's short term price pressure but no dilution. A dividend pays cash to shareholders and the price drops by the dividend on the ex date, so you're neutral on the day. A bonus or a split gives you free shares and the price falls in proportion, so your wealth is unchanged. A buyback cancels shares, pays the tendering shareholders, and is often supportive for the price. One tax note: since the 1st of October 2024, buyback proceeds are taxed as dividend income in the shareholder's hands.", "The takeaway: Dividends, bonuses and splits are value-neutral on the day; rights and FPOs can dilute; OFS moves shares between holders.", "Pause and answer: What happens to the price on a stock's ex-dividend date?", "⏸", "Answer: It drops by about the dividend; your wealth is unchanged."]], ["adjustment-arithmetic", ["The adjustment arithmetic. After a 1 for 1 bonus, the new price is the old price divided by 2. After a split, the new price is the old price divided by the split ratio. And on the ex dividend date, the open is roughly the previous close minus the dividend. Example: 10 shares at ₹2,900, worth ₹29,000, become 20 shares at ₹1,450 after a 1 for 1 bonus, still ₹29,000. A split from ₹10 face value to ₹1 turns one share into ten at a tenth of the price. A 1 for 5 rights issue at ₹200 when the market price is ₹300 gives your entitlement real value because of the ₹100 discount, so subscribe or sell it, but never ignore it. And under T plus 1, the ex date and record date usually coincide, so own the share before the ex date to get the dividend.", "The takeaway: Bonus and split divide the price by the ratio; ex-dividend opens about the dividend lower; total value is unchanged.", "Pause and answer: 10 shares at ₹2,900 after a 1:1 bonus?", "⏸", "Answer: 20 shares at ₹1,450, still ₹29,000."]], ["why-did-my-stock-drop-overnight", ["This ladder answers a common panic: why did my stock open sharply lower? First question: is it ex dividend today, and does the drop match the dividend? If yes, it's just a dividend adjustment; the cash is coming to you, and there's nothing to do. If not: is a bonus or split effective, and did your share count go up? If yes, it's a corporate action and your value is unchanged. If not: was a rights issue or FPO announced? If yes, it's dilution or rights pricing, and you should decide whether to subscribe. If none of these apply, it's real news, so read the exchange filings before you act.", "The takeaway: Check for ex-dividend, bonus or split, then rights or FPO, before assuming bad news.", "Pause and answer: The drop equals the dividend on the ex-date. What should you do?", "⏸", "Answer: Nothing: it's an adjustment, and the cash is coming to you."]]], "read": [[null, ["Section four: reading the market. Quotes, depth, circuits, indices and the jargon."]], ["anatomy-of-a-stock-quote", ["The anatomy of a stock quote. LTP, the last traded price, is the price of the most recent trade: a record, not a forecast. Previous close is yesterday's official close, the reference for \"change\". Change is LTP minus previous close, in rupees and percent. Bid and ask are the best price a buyer will pay and a seller will accept right now. The spread is ask minus bid: a spread of paise means liquid, rupees means thin. OHLC is the open, high, low and close, which together make one candlestick. And volume is the shares traded so far in the session. The change formula: LTP minus previous close, divided by previous close, times 100. From 1,468.50 to 1,476.30 is about plus 0.53%.", "The takeaway: LTP is the last trade, change is versus the previous close, bid and ask are live, and the spread shows liquidity.", "Pause and answer: Is LTP a forecast?", "⏸", "Answer: No. It's the price of the most recent trade."]], ["market-depth-the-five-best-bids-and-asks", ["Market depth shows the five best bids and asks. In the example, the best bid is 1,476.20 for 320 shares, and the best ask is 1,476.30 for 280. Total buy quantity is 3,280 against sell quantity of 3,240, roughly balanced. The left side shows demand below the price; the right side shows supply above it. But depth is a snapshot, not a crystal ball: large orders can be cancelled in an instant. And pay attention to volume: a price rise on high volume shows conviction, while a rise on thin volume is tentative.", "The takeaway: Depth shows the five best bids and asks; it's a snapshot, and large orders can vanish instantly.", "Pause and answer: Which side of the depth shows supply?", "⏸", "Answer: The ask side, above the price."]], ["circuits-daily-price-bands", ["Circuits are daily price bands. Individual stocks have bands of 2, 5, 10 or 20%, while large stocks with derivatives have wider, flexible bands and rarely lock. At the upper circuit only buyers are left and trading freezes. At the lower circuit only sellers are left, and holders may not be able to exit for days. There are also market wide circuit breakers: a 10, 15 or 20% fall in the Nifty or Sensex pauses all trading. Circuits trap both sides. Chasing an upper circuit stock can leave you unable to sell when it turns.", "The takeaway: Stocks have 2–20% daily bands; a lower circuit can trap holders for days; index breakers at 10, 15, 20% halt the whole market.", "Pause and answer: What happens at a lower circuit?", "⏸", "Answer: Only sellers remain, trading freezes, and holders may not exit for days."]], ["how-an-index-is-built", ["How an index is built. An index is one number for a weighted basket of stocks. You can't buy the index itself, but an index fund or ETF holds every constituent at its weight. Weight comes from free float market cap, meaning only shares available to the public count, not promoter or government holdings. The Nifty 50 on NSE and the Sensex on BSE, with 30 stocks, overlap heavily and usually move by similar percentages. Weights drift every day, and constituents are reviewed twice a year. A stock's contribution to the index move is its weight times its percentage move. A stock with a 10.6% weight rising 2% adds about 0.21% to the index, while a stock with a 0.3% weight rising 2% adds about 0.006%.", "The takeaway: An index weights stocks by free-float market cap; you own it through an index fund or ETF.", "Pause and answer: What does free-float market cap exclude?", "⏸", "Answer: Shares not available to the public, such as promoter or government holdings."]], ["the-nifty-is-top-heavy", ["The Nifty is top heavy. These weights are as the course gives them in 2026, and NSE updates its factsheets monthly. HDFC Bank is about 10.6% of it, ICICI Bank and Reliance about 8.3% each, Bharti Airtel about 5.2%, and Larsen and Toubro about 4.4%. The top 10 stocks make up about half the index, and financials about 35%. So \"the market fell\" often means a few heavyweights fell; check breadth before you conclude anything. Sector indices like Bank Nifty, Nifty IT, Auto, Pharma, FMCG and Metal show what's driving the day, and the Nifty 100, 500, midcap and smallcap indices show the wider market.", "The takeaway: The top 10 stocks are about half the Nifty and financials about a third, so 'the market fell' may mean a few heavyweights fell.", "Pause and answer: Roughly what share of the Nifty is the top 10?", "⏸", "Answer: About half."]], ["bull-bear-and-size-buckets", ["Some jargon. A bull market is a sustained rise. A bear market is a fall of 20% or more from a recent peak. A correction is a fall of about 10% or more, and a crash is a sudden, panic driven plunge. Breadth compares advancing with declining stocks; weak breadth means a few giants are hiding a falling market. Volatility is the size and speed of swings in either direction. SEBI ranks companies by market cap: large caps are the top 100 and steadier; mid caps rank 101 to 250, growth with more risk; small caps rank 251 and below, with the biggest swings both ways. And a penny stock's low price isn't a bargain. Liquidity matters most on the day you need to exit.", "The takeaway: A bear market is a 20% fall from a peak, a correction about 10%; SEBI ranks large, mid and small caps by market cap.", "Pause and answer: Correction versus bear market?", "⏸", "Answer: A correction is about 10% or more; a bear market 20% or more from a recent peak."]], ["long-vs-short", ["Long versus short. Going long means buying now and selling later: you profit if the price rises, and the worst case is losing what you paid. Going short means selling first and buying back later: you profit if the price falls, but the loss is unlimited because prices have no ceiling. In India, most retail shorts in the cash market are intraday only; holding a short overnight needs formal borrowing through SLB, securities lending and borrowing. The short profit per share is the sell price minus the buy back price: sell at 100, buy back at 80, and you make ₹20.", "The takeaway: Long profits from a rise with loss capped at what you paid; short profits from a fall with unlimited loss; retail shorts are mostly intraday.", "Pause and answer: Why is a short's loss unlimited?", "⏸", "Answer: Prices have no ceiling."]]], "moves": [[null, ["Section five: what moves prices. Expectations, macroeconomics and events."]], ["prices-move-on-surprise-not-on-news", ["Prices move on surprise, not on news. Price is the latest agreed trade, not fair value, and every tick is an imbalance between eager buyers and eager sellers. What tips the balance is news, earnings, expectations and market wide sentiment. Markets price the future, so results are judged against expectations. A stock expected to grow profit 15% that reports 12% can fall, even though the business grew. That's also why you hear \"buy the rumour, sell the news\": prices often run up before good news and slip once it arrives. Roughly, a beat often jumps, a meet is usually muted, and a miss often falls.", "The takeaway: Markets price the future, so results are judged against expectations: 12% growth against 15% expected can sink a stock.", "Pause and answer: Why can a stock fall on good results?", "⏸", "Answer: The results missed expectations already priced in."]], ["measuring-returns-properly", ["Measuring returns properly. Absolute return is selling price minus buying price. Percentage return is the gain divided by the buying price, times 100. A ₹100 gain on ₹400 is 25%, but the same ₹100 on ₹2,000 is only 5%. Always state the time too: doubling your money in 15 years is under 5% a year, below a typical FD. Use annualised returns to compare holdings with different holding periods.", "The takeaway: State returns as percentages with a time period; annualise to compare holdings.", "Pause and answer: ₹100 gain on ₹400 versus on ₹2,000?", "⏸", "Answer: 25% versus 5%."]], ["rates-inflation-and-the-rbi", ["Rates, inflation and the RBI. The repo rate is the price of money. Lower rates mean cheaper loans, more spending, money moving from FDs to equities, and a higher present value for future profits, so they're generally supportive for stocks. Higher rates do the reverse. The RBI targets about 4% inflation within a band of 2 to 6%. Hot inflation brings rate hikes; inflation well inside the band leaves room to cut. In 2025, for example, the repo rate was cut from about 6.5% to about 5.25% as retail inflation fell sharply.", "The takeaway: Lower repo rates generally support stocks; the RBI targets about 4% inflation in a 2–6% band.", "Pause and answer: Why do rate cuts generally help stocks?", "⏸", "Answer: Cheaper loans, more spending, money moving from FDs, and higher present value of future profits."]], ["fiis-vs-diis", ["FIIs versus DIIs. Foreign institutional or portfolio investors are foreign funds and pensions moving global capital. They're large, fast and driven by global cues, and when they sell, the index can drop sharply. Domestic institutional investors are mutual funds, insurers and pension funds investing domestic savings and SIP money. They're steadier, fed every month by SIPs, and they often cushion the fall. In 2025, record foreign selling of about ₹1.66 lakh crore was largely absorbed by domestic SIP inflows of about ₹31,000 crore a month, and domestic institutional ownership of NSE listed companies overtook foreign ownership.", "The takeaway: FIIs move large and fast on global cues; DIIs, fed by SIPs, are steadier and often cushion falls.", "Pause and answer: What absorbed record FII selling in 2025?", "⏸", "Answer: Domestic SIP inflows through DIIs."]], ["crude-rupee-and-inflation-chain", ["This chain shows why crude oil matters so much for India. Crude rises. India imports about 85% of its crude, so the import bill grows. That means more demand for dollars, and the rupee weakens. Imports cost more, so inflation rises. The RBI keeps rates higher. And that's a headwind for stocks. Note who wins and loses: a weaker rupee hurts importers like oil marketers and many manufacturers, and often helps dollar earners such as IT services and pharma.", "The takeaway: Higher crude raises India's import bill, weakens the rupee, lifts inflation and keeps rates high: a headwind for stocks.", "Pause and answer: Roughly what share of its crude does India import?", "⏸", "Answer: About 85%."]], ["global-cues-and-the-macro-mood-check", ["Global cues matter. A hawkish US Fed tends to pull money towards the US and away from emerging markets like India. Overnight US and Asian markets and GIFT Nifty hint at how India will open, and wars, oil shocks, elections and trade news spread within hours. The macro mood check is six questions. Are rates rising or falling? Is inflation under control? Is the rupee stable or sliding? Is crude calm or spiking? Are foreign investors net sellers or buyers? And is the world risk on or risk off? But macro is context, not a timing tool. Check it at turning points, then stick to your plan.", "The takeaway: Check rates, inflation, rupee, crude and FII flows; a hawkish Fed pulls money from emerging markets.", "Pause and answer: What indicator hints at India's open before 9:15?", "⏸", "Answer: GIFT Nifty (with overnight US and Asian markets)."]], ["results-season-and-dividend-dates", ["Results season. Listed companies report four times a year: Q1 is April to June, Q2 July to September, Q3 October to December, and Q4 January to March, and each season lasts about six weeks. Watch revenue, the top line; net profit, the bottom line; margins; growth year on year and quarter on quarter; and above all, guidance. A guidance cut can sink a stock despite good results. For dividends, there's the announcement, then the ex date, which you must own the stock before, then the record date. Dividend yield is the annual dividend divided by the share price, times 100: ₹20 on a ₹400 stock is 5%.", "The takeaway: Four results seasons a year; watch revenue, profit, margins and especially guidance; own the stock before the ex-date for the dividend.", "Pause and answer: Why can a guidance cut sink a stock with good results?", "⏸", "Answer: Markets price the future, and guidance changes expectations."]], ["catalysts-to-plan-around", ["Catalysts to plan around. Company specific ones include mergers and acquisitions, regulatory news, management changes, big orders, court rulings, rating changes, and index inclusion or exclusion, since index funds must buy or sell. Scheduled market wide events include the Union Budget on the 1st of February, election results and RBI policy. And unscheduled shocks include pandemics, wars, banking scares and surprise central bank moves. On results day prices can whip both ways within minutes, so learn from the calendar but don't trade the noise.", "The takeaway: Company events, scheduled market-wide events and unscheduled shocks all move prices; plan around the known ones.", "Pause and answer: Why does index inclusion move a stock?", "⏸", "Answer: Index funds must buy it."]]], "start": [[null, ["Section six: becoming an investor. First steps, mindset, and staying safe."]], ["opening-your-accounts", ["To invest you need three linked accounts: a bank account for money, a trading account to place orders, and a demat account to hold shares. Trading and demat are usually opened together online in a day or two. For KYC you'll need your PAN, Aadhaar with OTP verification, bank details, a photo and signature, and sometimes a video check, and then you e sign. But opening an account is the smallest milestone. Being ready means having an emergency fund, no costly debt, a simple plan and a steady SIP.", "The takeaway: Bank, trading and demat accounts, linked, with KYC done online; opening an account is the smallest milestone.", "Pause and answer: Name the three linked accounts.", "⏸", "Answer: Bank, trading and demat."]], ["index-sip-or-direct-stocks", ["Index SIP or direct stocks? With an index fund SIP you own a slice of the whole index, get instant diversification, need very little effort because it's automated, and your risk is spread out. It's best for most beginners. With direct stocks you own companies you pick, you build diversification yourself, the effort is high because you research each one, and the risk is concentrated. That's for later, once you can justify each holding. SIPs also bring automatic discipline and rupee cost averaging: a fixed amount buys more units when prices are low. Many start from just ₹500 a month.", "The takeaway: Most beginners should start with an index SIP: instant diversification, low effort, automatic discipline.", "Pause and answer: What's rupee-cost averaging?", "⏸", "Answer: A fixed amount buys more units when prices are low and fewer when high."]], ["beginner-roadmap", ["The beginner roadmap, in order. Build an emergency fund of 3 to 6 months' expenses somewhere safe and liquid. Clear costly debt like credit cards and expensive personal loans. Open demat and trading accounts and complete KYC. Start a small automated SIP in a broad index fund, ₹500 to ₹1,000 a month. Keep learning about what you own. Add direct stocks slowly, with a small slice of money you can afford to lose. And stay consistent, letting compounding work for years.", "The takeaway: Emergency fund, no costly debt, accounts, a small index SIP, learning, then direct stocks slowly with money you can afford to lose.", "Pause and answer: What's a reasonable first SIP amount?", "⏸", "Answer: Small, about ₹500–1,000 a month in a broad index fund."]], ["before-buying-any-single-stock", ["Before buying any single stock, ask yourself four things. Can I explain what the business does and how it makes money? Why could it be bigger or more profitable in 5 to 10 years? What could break that story? And how much of my portfolio will it be, and what will I do if it falls 50%? Borrowed conviction from tips and TV leads to panic selling at the first dip. Form your own view, or stay with the index.", "The takeaway: Explain the business, why it grows, what breaks the story, and your size and plan if it falls 50%.", "Pause and answer: Why does borrowed conviction from tips hurt?", "⏸", "Answer: It leads to panic-selling at the first dip."]], ["position-sizing-why-one-stock-can-t-sink-you", ["Position sizing is why one stock can't sink you. The portfolio hit equals the stock's weight times its fall. A stock that's 5% of your portfolio falling 50% costs you 2.5% of your wealth. A stock that's 80% of your portfolio falling 50% costs you 40%. Remember that higher return always means higher risk, and a \"guaranteed high return\" is either a misunderstanding or a scam. And expect markets to fall 30 to 50% now and then. That's normal, not a malfunction.", "The takeaway: Portfolio hit = weight × the stock's fall; small weights make one blow-up survivable.", "Pause and answer: A 5% position falls 50%. Portfolio hit?", "⏸", "Answer: 2.5%."]], ["the-emotional-cycle", ["The emotional cycle goes round like this: optimism, then euphoria, which is peak risk, then anxiety, fear, panic, and despair, which is often the best opportunity, then hope, and back to optimism. The key insight is that feelings run opposite to opportunity. Comfort is highest near market tops and lowest near bottoms.", "The takeaway: Optimism, euphoria, anxiety, fear, panic, despair, hope: feelings run opposite to opportunity.", "Pause and answer: At which stage is risk highest?", "⏸", "Answer: Euphoria."]], ["biases-that-cost-investors-money", ["Biases that cost investors money, and their counters. FOMO and herding: buying after a big rally because everyone else is. Counter it by automating your buying and ignoring tips. Loss aversion: holding losers and selling winners, sometimes called watering the weeds and cutting the flowers, because losses hurt about twice as much as gains please. Counter it by writing sell rules while calm. Anchoring: \"I'll sell when it gets back to ₹500.\" Judge today's business, not your cost. Overconfidence: bigger bets and more trades after a few wins. Use fixed position size limits. Recency: expecting good years to repeat, or avoiding stocks after a crash. Keep a long horizon and a steady SIP. Studies like Morningstar's \"Mind the Gap\" find the average fund investor earns roughly 1 to 1.5 points a year less than their own funds, because of badly timed buying and selling. Habits that help: automate, write down why you own each holding, review monthly or quarterly, and do nothing for a day after a big win or loss.", "The takeaway: FOMO, loss aversion, anchoring and overconfidence cost money; counter them with automation and rules written while calm.", "Pause and answer: What is anchoring?", "⏸", "Answer: Fixating on a past price, such as 'I'll sell when it's back to ₹500', instead of judging today's business."]], ["india-s-big-market-scams-and-what-changed", ["India's big market scams, and what changed after each. Harshad Mehta in 1992 used fake bank receipts to fund a stock ramp of more than ₹4,000 crore, which led to SEBI's statutory powers, electronic trading on NSE, and demat. Ketan Parekh in 2001 pumped a group of stocks through circular trading with misused bank money, which brought tighter surveillance and the badla ban. At Satyam in 2009, the founder inflated profits, including about ₹5,000 crore of cash that didn't exist, leading to stronger audit and governance rules. NSEL in 2013 defaulted on about ₹5,600 crore of commodity contracts backed by goods that barely existed, and spot exchange oversight was overhauled. And Karvy in 2019 pledged about ₹2,000 crore of clients' shares without consent, after which client securities were ring fenced and pledge rules tightened.", "The takeaway: Harshad Mehta, Ketan Parekh and Satyam each led to reforms: SEBI powers, demat, surveillance and governance.", "Pause and answer: What did the Satyam fraud involve?", "⏸", "Answer: The founder inflated profits, including about ₹5,000 crore of fictitious cash."]], ["modern-traps", ["Modern traps. Pump and dump: operators buy a cheap illiquid stock, hype it in WhatsApp or Telegram groups, then sell into the buying they created. Finfluencers and fake advisers: paid tips without SEBI registration are illegal, and registered firms may not tie up with unregistered finfluencers. Guaranteed return apps: early withdrawals work, new deposits pay old ones, and then the app vanishes. And dabba trading: \"trades\" that never reach an exchange, a criminal offence with no legal protection for you. Know the two registrations. A Research Analyst publishes research views. A Registered Investment Adviser gives personal, fee based advice and owes a duty to you.", "The takeaway: Pump-and-dump groups, unregistered finfluencers and guaranteed-return apps are today's common traps.", "Pause and answer: How does a pump-and-dump work?", "⏸", "Answer: Operators buy a cheap illiquid stock, hype it in chat groups, then sell into the buying they created."]], ["is-this-tip-a-scam", ["This ladder checks whether a tip is a scam. First: does it promise guaranteed or fixed high returns? If yes, walk away; real markets never guarantee returns. If not: is the person not registered with SEBI, or without a registration number you can verify? If so, walk away, because unregistered paid advice is illegal. If they are registered: do they push urgency, an obscure small cap, or ask for OTPs or logins? If yes, that's a red flag for a classic pump and dump or an account takeover. And even if it passes all three, still ask who gains if you buy, and do your own homework.", "The takeaway: Guaranteed returns, no verifiable SEBI registration, urgency, obscure small caps or OTP requests mean walk away.", "Pause and answer: A tip promises fixed high returns. What does the ladder say?", "⏸", "Answer: Walk away: real markets never guarantee returns."]], ["if-you-have-been-defrauded", ["If you've been defrauded, act fast. Stop all further payments immediately. Save screenshots, chats and transaction records. If a registered entity is involved, complain on SEBI's SCORES portal. Report cyber fraud on the helpline 1930 or at cybercrime.gov.in. Speed matters, because frozen money can sometimes be recovered.", "The takeaway: Stop payments, save evidence, complain on SEBI SCORES for registered entities, and report cyber fraud on 1930 fast.", "Pause and answer: Which helpline handles cyber fraud?", "⏸", "Answer: 1930 (or cybercrime.gov.in)."]]], "last": [[null, ["The final section is checklists and formulas. Run these before your first investment, before any single stock, and whenever a tip arrives."]], ["before-you-start-investing", ["Before you start investing, check six things. An emergency fund of 3 to 6 months is in place. You have no credit card or expensive personal loan debt. Your demat, trading and bank accounts are linked and KYC is done. You've chosen a monthly SIP amount you can keep paying even in bad years. Money you need within 3 to 5 years is kept out of equities. And you judge returns after inflation and tax.", "The takeaway: Cushion, no costly debt, linked accounts, a sustainable SIP, short-term money out of equity, returns judged after inflation and tax.", "Pause and answer: Money needed within 3–5 years: equity or not?", "⏸", "Answer: Not; keep it out of equities."]], ["before-buying-a-stock", ["Before buying a stock: you can explain the business and why it grows. You know what's already expected and priced in. The position size survives a 50% fall. You've checked the spread, volume and circuit band. You've checked the corporate action calendar. And your sell rules are written down.", "The takeaway: Know the business, what's priced in, a size that survives a 50% fall, liquidity, the corporate-action calendar and written sell rules.", "Pause and answer: Why check the circuit band before buying?", "⏸", "Answer: A stock with a narrow band can lock and trap you."]], ["staying-safe", ["Staying safe. Deal only with SEBI registered brokers, advisers and funds, and verify them on sebi.gov.in. Never share passwords, OTPs or remote access. Ignore \"sure shot\" tips and guaranteed returns. Keep shares in your own demat and read your contract notes. And always ask who gains if you buy.", "The takeaway: Use SEBI-registered entities, never share OTPs or passwords, ignore guaranteed tips, keep shares in your own demat.", "Pause and answer: Where do you verify a broker or adviser's registration?", "⏸", "Answer: sebi.gov.in."]], ["one-line-formulas", ["The one line formulas, as a quick review. Real return is roughly nominal return minus inflation. Ownership percentage is your shares divided by shares outstanding. Implied valuation is the investment divided by the stake. Listing gain percentage is listing price minus issue price, divided by issue price, times 100. A stock's index contribution is its weight times its percentage move. Dividend yield is the annual dividend divided by price, times 100. And the portfolio hit from one stock is its weight times its fall.", "The takeaway: Real return ≈ nominal − inflation; dividend yield = dividend ÷ price; portfolio hit = weight × fall.", "Pause and answer: Dividend yield formula?", "⏸", "Answer: Annual dividend ÷ price × 100."]], ["about-this-sheet", ["This sheet condenses the free Stock Market Basics course from OpenAlgo Open Varsity, 18 chapters in 6 modules, into reference cards written in our own words. Each section links back to its chapters for the full explanations, charts and stories. Figures are approximate and dated, and tax, SEBI and exchange rules change, so check the current versions. It's educational, not investment advice. That's the whole sheet. Thanks for listening, and happy learning."]], ["numbers-that-expire", ["The numbers that expire card collects every figure on this sheet that a regulator, exchange or budget can change, like lot sizes, tax rates, STT and expiry days. Each one shows its current value and when it last changed, with a link to the source. Use it as a checklist: before you trade on any number from this sheet, check it there, and then check the source, because these move."]]]};
